Title:
ObRegisterCallbacks and countermeasures

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post reverse engineers how Windows kernel object callbacks registered via `ObRegisterCallbacks` are stored and invoked, focusing on how AV/anti-cheat drivers use them to restrict process/thread handle access.  
- It documents the (then) undocumented internal callback structures (`CALLBACK_ENTRY`, `CALLBACK_ENTRY_ITEM`) and shows where callbacks live: the `OBJECT_TYPE->CallbackList` for `PsProcessType` and `PsThreadType`.  
- The author explains how to enumerate registered callbacks by walking these lists, recover the “handle” (actually a pointer to `CALLBACK_ENTRY`), and disable callbacks either by calling `ObUnRegisterCallbacks` or by directly patching callback function pointers.  
- A key issue is stability: unregistering frees memory, so the original driver later unregistering its callback can bugcheck due to a stale pointer; the proposed workaround is DKOM-style pointer replacement (dummy pre/post callbacks) instead of removal.  
- It also discusses countermeasures anti-cheat/AV might use (altitude re-registration checks, integrity walking of callback lists) and suggests spoofing altitude collisions by registering a replacement callback at the same altitude.  
- Useful for kernel researchers, red teamers/pentesters doing Windows internals work, and defenders wanting to understand how handle-operation filtering is implemented and how it can be tampered with.

Technical Focus:
- Windows kernel Object Manager callbacks (`ObRegisterCallbacks`, `ObUnRegisterCallbacks`)
- `OBJECT_TYPE` internals and `CallbackList` enumeration (PsProcessType/PsThreadType)
- Undocumented callback storage structures (`CALLBACK_ENTRY`, `CALLBACK_ENTRY_ITEM`)
- Altitude ordering and `STATUS_FLT_INSTANCE_ALTITUDE_COLLISION`
- DKOM-style patching of pre/post operation callback pointers
- Anti-cheat/AV tamper detection considerations

Use Cases:
- Enumerate and analyze kernel-registered process/thread handle filtering callbacks
- Disable or neutralize anti-cheat/EDR handle restrictions for process memory access (research context)
- Build detections for suspicious modification of `OBJECT_TYPE->CallbackList` or callback function pointers
- Validate robustness of kernel drivers that rely on Ob callbacks (unload/unregister safety)

Keywords:
ObRegisterCallbacks, ObUnRegisterCallbacks, Object Manager, OB_CALLBACK_REGISTRATION, OB_OPERATION_REGISTRATION, PsProcessType, PsThreadType, OBJECT_TYPE, CallbackList, CALLBACK_ENTRY, CALLBACK_ENTRY_ITEM, PreOperationCallback, PostOperationCallback, altitude, STATUS_FLT_INSTANCE_ALTITUDE_COLLISION, DKOM, kernel driver, anti-cheat, Battleye, EasyAntiCheat, PatchGuard, Windows 7, handle access filtering